Welcome to notebook three — vector spaces. In the previous notebook we used two operations on vectors: addition and scalar multiplication. A vector space is just a set of objects, called vectors, that is closed under these two operations. Meaning, if you take any two vectors in the set and add them, you stay in the set. And if you scale any vector in the set by a number, you also stay in the set.

The canonical vector space is R n, all real valued lists of length n. R two is the plane. R three is three dimensional space. R seven eight four is the space that m nist digit images live in.

This sounds abstract, but the payoff is concrete. Once we know we are inside a vector space, we get to ask the three questions this notebook is really about. First, what can I reach by combining these vectors? That is the span. Second, are any of these vectors redundant? That is linear independence. Third, what is the minimum number of vectors I need to describe everything? That is basis and dimension.

These three questions are the entire vocabulary of feature engineering, embedding design, and dimensionality reduction in machine learning.

Let us start with span. The span of a set of vectors is the set of all linear combinations of those vectors.

If you have a single vector v, its span is the line through the origin in the direction of v. Every multiple of v lies on this line.

If you have two vectors u and v that point in different directions, their span is the entire plane through the origin that contains both arrows.

But if u and v happen to point in the same direction, meaning one is a scalar multiple of the other, then their span is still just a line. Adding a redundant vector does not enlarge the span.

In R three, three vectors usually span all of R three, the whole three dimensional space. But if all three happen to lie in the same plane, their span is just that plane.

This is the central question of span. Given these vectors, what is the geometric shape — line, plane, or full space — that I can reach with them?

Next, linear independence. A set of vectors is called linearly independent if the only way to write the zero vector as a linear combination of them is the trivial way, where every coefficient is zero. If you can find some non zero combination that hits zero, the set is linearly dependent, and at least one of the vectors is redundant. That redundant vector can be written as a combination of the others.

The intuition. Take v one equals one comma two and v two equals two comma four. Notice that two times v one plus negative one times v two equals zero. The coefficients are not all zero, so the set is dependent. Geometrically, v two is just v one scaled — it adds nothing new.

There is a useful shortcut. Stack the vectors as columns of a matrix and compute the rank. The rank counts how many linearly independent vectors are in the set. If the rank equals the number of vectors you have, the set is independent. If the rank is smaller, the set is dependent, and the number of redundant vectors is the difference.

Why does this matter in machine learning? When two features in your dataset are linearly dependent, your model has no way to assign unique weights to each. The matrix x transpose x becomes singular. Ridge regression's lambda times identity term was invented to fix exactly this problem. And the entire job of principal component analysis is to find a non redundant set of directions in your data.

Now we come to the basis. A basis for a vector space is a set of vectors with two properties. First, the set is linearly independent — no redundancy. Second, the set spans the whole space — you can reach every vector by combining them. In short, a basis is the smallest set you need to describe the entire space.

The standard basis of R two is the pair e one equals one comma zero, and e two equals zero comma one. Any vector a comma b is just a times e one plus b times e two. The standard basis of R n follows the same pattern. There are n basis vectors, each with a single one and the rest zeros.

But the standard basis is just one choice. Any linearly independent set of n vectors in R n is also a basis. For example, b one equals two comma one, and b two equals one comma three, is also a basis for R two. They are independent because they are not parallel, and they span the plane.

Here is the key insight. Coordinates depend on the basis. When you write a vector as three comma four, you implicitly mean three of e one plus four of e two. In a different basis, the same point has different coordinates. Principal component analysis, the fourier transform, and wavelet transforms are all really just one move — express the data in a smarter basis. This is one of the most important moves in all of machine learning. Keep the data, change the basis.

Next, dimension. The dimension of a vector space is the number of vectors in any basis for it. Any basis at all — every basis of R two has exactly two vectors, every basis of R three has exactly three, every basis of R n has exactly n. Dimension is an intrinsic property of the space, not of any particular basis.

A few useful facts follow. You need at least n vectors to span R n. With fewer than n, your span is only a proper subspace, a line or a plane or similar. You can have at most n independent vectors in R n. Any larger set must contain a linear dependence. And a set of exactly n vectors in R n is a basis if and only if it is linearly independent, which is equivalent to saying the determinant is not zero.

In machine learning, the word dimension gets used in two different ways. The ambient dimension is the number of features in your data. M nist images have ambient dimension seven hundred and eighty four. The intrinsic dimension is the dimension of the often much smaller subspace where the data actually lives. M nist images live near a much lower dimensional manifold, and principal component analysis tries to estimate that. The gap between ambient and intrinsic dimension is why dimensionality reduction works at all.

Now subspaces. A subspace of R n is a subset that is itself a vector space — meaning it is closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Geometrically, a subspace of R n is always one of just a few things. The trivial subspace containing only the zero vector, which has dimension zero. A line through the origin, dimension one. A plane through the origin, dimension two. And so on, up to all of R n itself, dimension n.

There is an important subtlety. Subspaces must pass through the origin. A line that does not go through the origin is not a subspace, because scaling any point on it by zero gives the origin, which is then not on the line.

Two subspaces matter so much that they get their own notebook later. The column space of a matrix a is the span of its columns. This is the set of all vectors b for which the system a x equals b has a solution. The null space of a matrix a is the set of all vectors x such that a x equals zero. This measures how much of the input space the matrix collapses to zero. Together with two more subspaces, these form Gilbert Strang's four fundamental subspaces, the cleanest summary of what a matrix does.

Let us close with where you see span, independence, basis, and dimension in machine learning. These four ideas underlie every dimensionality reduction and feature engineering decision you will make.

Feature redundancy, also called multicollinearity. If two features in your dataset are linearly dependent, your design matrix has columns that do not bring new information. X transpose x becomes singular, and you cannot solve the normal equations directly. This is why ridge regression's lambda i term exists.

Principal component analysis. Finds a new orthonormal basis for your data, ordered by variance. The first k basis vectors span the best k dimensional subspace approximation to your dataset.

Word and image embeddings. Each embedding is a vector in some R d. The embedding space is itself a vector space, and similar concepts cluster into low dimensional subspaces inside it.

Latent variables and bottleneck layers. Autoencoders compress high dimensional input into a low dimensional latent code. They are explicitly learning a basis for the data's intrinsic subspace.

Rank based regularization. Low rank matrix factorization, used in recommender systems and in lora fine tuning of large language models, constrains weight matrices to have small rank — meaning the matrix lies in a low dimensional subspace.

Underdetermined systems. When you have more features than samples, the normal equations have infinitely many solutions, an entire subspace of valid weight vectors. Regularization picks one specific solution from that subspace.

The next notebook covers matrices. Once we have vectors and the vector spaces they live in, a matrix becomes a function between those spaces. That single perspective unlocks the rest of linear algebra.
